1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:26,020
Okay, so we're back at the screen. We're
now going to look at generating
international trade ideas in individual
equities, like we did in the previous
video using the ISM, but now we're going
to be looking at ADRs, American Depository
Receipts, which are essentially
international companies, so non-domestic
U.S. companies.

2
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:51,680
So we're looking at companies outside the
United States with listings on U.S.
exchanges as ADRs. Now this video is going
to be relatively shorter than most videos
in the PTM video series, and that's
because this is really just an exercise in
organization, common sense, and
processing.

3
00:00:51,680 --> 00:01:04,100
Now, if you recall, the macro dashboard,
essentially what we're doing here is we're
looking at the right-hand side now of the
macroeconomic leading indicator dashboard.

4
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:21,960
When it comes to American Depository
Receipts, so international companies with
U.S. dollar-denominated stock listings on
U.S. domestic exchanges, when it comes to
ADRs, there is a universe of ADRs.

5
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:42,460
At any one time, there's going to be a
certain amount of ADRs that are currently
listed on U.S. exchanges, and of course,
those stocks, those companies, will have
exposure to different geographies around
the world.

6
00:01:43,240 --> 00:02:05,120
However, their ADR listings mean they
report in U.S. dollars, and similarly,
even U.S. companies that have
international exposure in their revenues
and earnings will have exposure to certain
international geographies outside the
United States.

7
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:22,560
So whether a company is listed in the U.S.
as an international ADR, denominated in
U.S. dollars, or it's a U.S. company with
international exposure, regardless, if a
company has international exposure,

8
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:34,660
we, of course, need to have resources to
enable us to appraise the international
exposure in establishing a portfolio bias.

9
00:02:35,420 --> 00:02:48,920
So what we'll be doing in this video is
looking at this in the context of ADRs, so
not U.S. domestic companies with
international exposure, but the same
disciplines would apply.

10
00:02:49,780 --> 00:03:08,900
So, for example, a German company has an
ADR listing, and they have large exposure
with their revenues and earnings in
Germany, maybe also in Italy, maybe also
across the rest of the European Union.

11
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:32,220
So, of course, we need to be on top of
German, Italian, and European
macroeconomics in order to have a
portfolio bias, a bias on German stocks
that have exposure, or companies that have
exposure predominantly to the German
economy, plus the Italian economy, and
even the wider European economy.

12
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:49,120
So if we are establishing a country bias,
and therefore potentially a stock bias, in
stocks that have exposure to certain
geographies, we need to be on top of a
number of things from a macro perspective.

13
00:03:49,800 --> 00:04:10,120
So we want to be on top of our money
market indicators in that particular
geography, we want to be on top of any
surveys, and we also want to be on top of
international cyclical commodity prices,
and whether we're seeing demand-led or
supply-led price increases or decreases.

14
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:26,440
So, yes, we use our macroeconomic leading
indicator dashboard to get an overall
portfolio bias, but we do also use the
macro dashboard in getting a geographical
bias as well.

15
00:04:26,760 --> 00:04:28,560
It can also be used for that.

16
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:47,600
And on top of that, it can also be used
for generating trade ideas in individual
equities, not only domestically in the
U.S., so for U.S.-listed domestic stocks,
but also for international ADRs.

17
00:04:48,160 --> 00:05:00,440
So what we're going to focus on here,
because we've already covered
international surveys in Europe and China,
we're going to focus here on the IHS
market PMIs.

18
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:10,560
So on the macro dashboard, you'll see the
IHS market PMIs mentioned for Japan, U.K.,
Brazil, and Canada.

19
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:16,940
And there is actually, in fact, a market
PMI index for most countries.

20
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:27,520
Now, we also mentioned earlier in the
video series about these things called
American Depository Receipts, ADRs, when
we were actually discussing China.

21
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:32,260
What ADRs actually are, are essentially
secondary listings.

22
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:40,740
So it's companies that have their primary
listing in another country denominated in
the currency of that country.

23
00:05:41,840 --> 00:05:55,680
And what a lot of companies actually do is
also choose to list equity in the U.S.
domestic market as American depository
receipts denominated in U.S. dollars.

24
00:05:55,680 --> 00:06:07,040
And this is usually so institutional
investors in the U.S., so most
importantly, U.S. pension money, can
maintain custody holdings onshore.

25
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:12,020
So it's lower risk, and it's in, well,
denominated in U.S. dollars.

26
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:20,500
And it's a win-win for both sides, because
the pension funds, U.S. pension funds, get
to invest in these companies,

27
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:24,660
and the companies get to raise capital in
the United States.

28
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:33,180
Now, if you recall from earlier in the
video series, when we covered ADRs, when
looking at China, we covered them briefly
there.

29
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:41,340
ADRs can trade on exchange, and they can
trade off exchange, so over-the-counter
OTC.

30
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:49,960
What we're interested in, as traders and
portfolio managers, we're interested in
only the on-exchange ADRs,

31
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:54,600
so the ones that we can access and trade
on our brokerage platforms.

32
00:06:55,600 --> 00:07:05,720
On top of that, many of these ADRs have
listed options also, but some don't, so we
always have to be aware of that.

33
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:14,940
It's basically a function of market
capitalization or size of the company and
the liquidity of the stock.

34
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:24,920
Now, up to this point, we've been looking
at a universe of around 2,200 stocks in
the U.S. domestically,

35
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:33,020
