123
00:19:14,180 --> 00:19:18,940
So let's start with the pharmaceutical and
biotech sector.

124
00:19:19,860 --> 00:19:25,840
And from a trader and portfolio manager
perspective, especially for retail,

125
00:19:26,460 --> 00:19:29,020
this is a very important sector to
discuss.

126
00:19:29,720 --> 00:19:34,920
Not just in the context of ADRs, but also
in the wider context,

127
00:19:35,480 --> 00:19:40,300
to include all pharma and biotech stocks
in the United States as well.

128
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:47,200
So in our ADR opportunity set,
pharmaceutical and biotech stocks,

129
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:54,840
even though it's the largest opportunity
set of any sector in the foreign ADRs
list,

130
00:19:55,300 --> 00:19:57,740
and it does make up 64 stocks,

131
00:19:58,020 --> 00:20:04,720
we actually don't recommend that you trade
them at least in your first year as a
retail trader,

132
00:20:04,960 --> 00:20:06,740
and perhaps even longer.

133
00:20:07,780 --> 00:20:12,900
And this would also go for US pharma and
biotech stocks as well.

134
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:14,540
Now, why is this?

135
00:20:14,760 --> 00:20:21,680
Well, it's because it's probably the most
difficult sector in the entire stock
market

136
00:20:21,680 --> 00:20:26,140
to actually have an edge in, even as a
professional trader.

137
00:20:27,260 --> 00:20:34,780
When you look at the individuals and
organizations or institutional investors
and traders

138
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:38,260
that are involved in this sector at the
highest level,

139
00:20:38,740 --> 00:20:41,400
so if we're looking at hedge funds and
investment banks,

140
00:20:41,440 --> 00:20:49,540
they have teams of PhD biochemists as both
research analysts and portfolio managers

141
00:20:49,540 --> 00:20:53,960
that have literally studied chemical
compounds for years.

142
00:20:54,840 --> 00:21:00,380
And the way pharmaceutical and biotech
stocks work and how they're valued,

143
00:21:00,940 --> 00:21:04,260
well, they have both current drug
portfolios,

144
00:21:04,820 --> 00:21:09,620
so drugs that are being sold in the market
right now and making current sales,

145
00:21:10,180 --> 00:21:11,900
and they have pipeline drugs,

146
00:21:12,340 --> 00:21:14,640
so drugs that are not currently being
sold,

147
00:21:15,240 --> 00:21:18,520
but they're going through the approval
process with the FDA.

148
00:21:18,840 --> 00:21:24,480
So they're going through phase one, phase
two, and phase three approval with the
FDA.

149
00:21:25,480 --> 00:21:31,000
Now, the fair value or NPV of these
companies' stock prices

150
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:35,160
is usually worked out on a per share basis

151
00:21:35,160 --> 00:21:40,480
by adding the DCF, the discounted cash
flow model of the current portfolio,

152
00:21:41,060 --> 00:21:47,360
together with a probability-adjusted DCF
of the pipeline portfolio,

153
00:21:47,720 --> 00:21:51,020
and then we arrive at an NPV per share.

154
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:55,680
Now, splitting this up into current and
pipeline,

155
00:21:55,680 --> 00:22:00,920
the current portfolio will include in
research analyst models

156
00:22:00,920 --> 00:22:06,320
an assumption of when each drug reaches
peak sales

157
00:22:06,320 --> 00:22:09,060
and when those drugs come off patent.

158
00:22:10,380 --> 00:22:15,080
And in the pipeline portfolio, so drugs
that are not yet approved,

159
00:22:15,780 --> 00:22:18,660
at different phases of the approval
process,

160
00:22:19,240 --> 00:22:21,640
so phase one, phase two, and phase three,

161
00:22:22,060 --> 00:22:26,760
at the FDA approval process for the
pipeline drugs,

162
00:22:27,300 --> 00:22:31,520
what analysts do is they assign
probabilities of approval

163
00:22:31,520 --> 00:22:36,200
that grow higher at each stage of the
clinical trials.

164
00:22:37,360 --> 00:22:40,020
So, from a retail trader perspective,

165
00:22:40,820 --> 00:22:45,660
I mean, there's pretty much no way that
you can have a real edge here

166
00:22:45,660 --> 00:22:50,220
over many trades in this sector over a
long period of time,

167
00:22:50,640 --> 00:22:53,880
unless, of course, you're a PhD biochemist

168
00:22:53,880 --> 00:22:55,960
and an absolute finance whiz,

169
00:22:56,360 --> 00:22:59,060
or you actually have inside information.

170
00:23:00,100 --> 00:23:03,800
So, yes, the tradable ADR's opportunity
set

171
00:23:03,800 --> 00:23:07,800
does increase our overall opportunity set

172
00:23:07,800 --> 00:23:09,380
in the stock market.

173
00:23:09,380 --> 00:23:15,720
However, when we get into the details of
the sectors themselves,

174
00:23:16,060 --> 00:23:20,020
the biggest one, pharmaceutical and
biotech stocks,

175
00:23:20,200 --> 00:23:24,040
is not very conducive over a long period
of time

176
00:23:24,040 --> 00:23:26,560
for retail traders to actually have an
edge.

